1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,346
Your dishwasher costs about twenty dollars a year to run.

2
00:00:03,646 --> 00:00:06,230
Your air conditioner? About five hundred.

3
00:00:06,830 --> 00:00:09,397
So where does your electric bill actually go?

4
00:00:09,697 --> 00:00:15,419
I ranked fifteen things in the average American home by what they cost to run for a full year.

5
00:00:15,719 --> 00:00:21,240
The numbers come from the federal government's own household energy survey, not from guesses.

6
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:28,663
For scale, the average American home buys about ten thousand eight hundred kilowatt-hours of electricity a year.

7
00:00:28,963 --> 00:00:32,882
At twenty twenty-six prices, that's almost two thousand dollars.

8
00:00:33,182 --> 00:00:36,287
And a few spots on this list will probably surprise you.

9
00:00:36,587 --> 00:00:40,773
First, a quick word on the math, so you know where every number comes from.

10
00:00:41,073 --> 00:00:45,781
The survey measures how many kilowatt-hours each appliance uses in a year.

11
00:00:46,081 --> 00:00:49,378
And it only counts homes that actually have that appliance.

12
00:00:49,678 --> 00:00:57,665
Then I multiplied that by what Americans are paying for power in twenty twenty-six: about eighteen cents per kilowatt-hour.

13
00:00:57,965 --> 00:01:00,149
That price has jumped since the survey.

14
00:01:00,449 --> 00:01:07,002
Running the exact same fridge costs about thirty-eight percent more today than it did in twenty twenty.

15
00:01:07,302 --> 00:01:12,714
Your own bill will depend on your climate and your rates. But this is the national picture.

16
00:01:13,014 --> 00:01:14,310
Let's start at the bottom.

17
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:16,838
Number fifteen: the washing machine.

18
00:01:17,138 --> 00:01:21,998
The machine itself uses only about thirteen dollars of electricity a year.

19
00:01:22,298 --> 00:01:24,457
But that number is a little misleading.

20
00:01:24,757 --> 00:01:32,068
According to Energy Star, about ninety percent of the energy it takes to run a washer goes into heating the water.

21
00:01:32,368 --> 00:01:36,096
And that cost doesn't vanish. It shows up later on this list.

22
00:01:36,396 --> 00:01:41,707
Energy Star also says switching from hot to warm can cut that energy in half.

23
00:01:42,007 --> 00:01:46,148
Number fourteen: the microwave. About nineteen dollars a year.

24
00:01:46,448 --> 00:01:51,252
It pulls a lot of power while it runs, but it only runs for minutes at a time.

25
00:01:51,552 --> 00:01:54,953
Number thirteen: the dishwasher, at about twenty dollars.

26
00:01:55,253 --> 00:02:01,529
Just like the washer, a big part of its real cost is the hot water it pulls from your water heater.

27
00:02:01,829 --> 00:02:08,692
Number twelve: ceiling fans. About forty-one dollars a year, for all the fans in a home that has them.

28
00:02:08,992 --> 00:02:12,457
Keep that number in mind for when we get to air conditioning.

29
00:02:12,757 --> 00:02:17,356
Number eleven: the electric stove and oven. About fifty-three dollars.

30
00:02:17,656 --> 00:02:20,180
Around four dollars and change per month.

31
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:25,392
That's the range and the oven only. The survey counts the microwave separately.

32
00:02:25,692 --> 00:02:27,571
Now the list gets more interesting.

33
00:02:28,071 --> 00:02:32,528
Number ten is something a lot of people own and completely forget about.

34
00:02:32,828 --> 00:02:36,194
The second refrigerator. About ninety dollars a year.

35
00:02:36,494 --> 00:02:40,978
The extra fridge in the garage or the basement, usually holding drinks.

36
00:02:41,278 --> 00:02:45,456
Your main fridge, by comparison, costs about a hundred and twelve.

37
00:02:45,756 --> 00:02:49,643
So the backup costs almost as much as the one you open every day.

38
00:02:49,943 --> 00:02:55,167
If it's mostly empty, unplugging it is one of the easiest savings in the whole house.

39
00:02:55,467 --> 00:03:01,612
Over ten years, at today's prices, that second fridge adds up to about nine hundred dollars.

40
00:03:01,912 --> 00:03:07,393
Number nine: a separate chest or upright freezer. About a hundred and four dollars.

41
00:03:07,693 --> 00:03:11,836
Almost nine dollars a month, just to keep frozen food frozen.

42
00:03:12,136 --> 00:03:15,848
Number eight: the main refrigerator, at a hundred and twelve.

43
00:03:16,148 --> 00:03:20,789
It runs every hour of every day, and it still lands in the middle of this list.

44
00:03:21,089 --> 00:03:24,704
Number seven: your TVs, and everything plugged into them.

45
00:03:25,004 --> 00:03:30,913
The survey counts cable boxes, streaming sticks, game consoles and sound systems too.

46
00:03:31,213 --> 00:03:34,484
All together, about a hundred and fifteen dollars a year.

47
00:03:34,784 --> 00:03:36,953
That's a little under ten dollars a month.

48
00:03:37,253 --> 00:03:40,948
Number six: lighting. About a hundred and nineteen dollars.

49
00:03:41,248 --> 00:03:44,755
And that's every bulb in the house, combined, for a whole year.

50
00:03:45,055 --> 00:03:48,115
Roughly ten dollars a month to light your entire home.

51
00:03:48,415 --> 00:03:52,068
Number five: the clothes dryer, at a hundred and twenty-four.

52
00:03:52,368 --> 00:03:57,806
It's the priciest laundry appliance by far, because its main job is making heat.

53
00:03:58,106 --> 00:04:01,364
That's more than nine times what the washer costs to run.

54
00:04:01,664 --> 00:04:02,899
And now a big jump.

55
00:04:03,199 --> 00:04:08,510
Number four costs more than your main fridge, and most people never even think about it.

56
00:04:08,810 --> 00:04:12,849
The dehumidifier. About a hundred and eighty-two dollars a year.

57
00:04:13,149 --> 00:04:15,539
That's around fifteen dollars every month.

58
00:04:15,839 --> 00:04:20,078
That's over sixty percent more than the refrigerator you use every day.

59
00:04:20,378 --> 00:04:24,431
In a damp basement, one can run for hours a day, all summer long.

60
00:04:24,731 --> 00:04:30,270
If you have one, check the humidity setting. Running it drier than you need just costs money.

61
00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:33,179
The top three are in a completely different league.

62
00:04:33,679 --> 00:04:38,506
Each one costs about half as much as the other twelve items on this list combined.

63
00:04:38,806 --> 00:04:43,912
Number three: the electric water heater. About four hundred and ninety-two dollars.

64
00:04:44,212 --> 00:04:48,299
Remember the washing machine? This is where its hot water shows up.

65
00:04:48,599 --> 00:04:51,870
Showers, dishes and laundry all get billed right here.

66
00:04:52,170 --> 00:04:56,026
That's about forty-one dollars a month, just to have hot water.

67
00:04:56,326 --> 00:05:02,160
Which is why washing clothes in cold water is one of the easiest ways to shrink this number.

68
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:10,030
The Department of Energy says heat pump water heaters can be two to three times more efficient than standard electric ones.

69
00:05:10,330 --> 00:05:17,818
By my math, that could bring this cost down to somewhere between one hundred sixty-five and two hundred fifty dollars a year.

70
00:05:18,118 --> 00:05:23,795
Number two: electric space heating, including the fan that pushes the warm air around.

71
00:05:24,095 --> 00:05:27,145
That's about four hundred and ninety-four dollars a year.

72
00:05:27,445 --> 00:05:35,304
This is the one that swings most with climate. In the Midwest, the average heating use is about twenty-five percent higher.

73
00:05:35,604 --> 00:05:41,056
Including the fan, that's about six hundred and forty-four dollars a year in the Midwest.

74
00:05:41,356 --> 00:05:42,150
And number one.

75
00:05:42,750 --> 00:05:47,848
Air conditioning, including its fan. About five hundred and five dollars a year.

76
00:05:48,148 --> 00:05:51,221
That's more than twelve times what your ceiling fans cost.

77
00:05:51,521 --> 00:05:54,270
Or about forty-two dollars every single month.

78
00:05:54,570 --> 00:05:58,428
But that's the national average, and where you live changes everything.

79
00:05:58,728 --> 00:06:04,104
In the Northeast, air conditioning averages about three hundred and thirteen dollars a year.

80
00:06:04,404 --> 00:06:07,210
Across the South, it's about seven hundred and eight.

81
00:06:07,510 --> 00:06:13,273
And in Texas, Oklahoma, Arkansas and Louisiana, about eight hundred and thirty-one.

82
00:06:13,573 --> 00:06:19,550
And notice how close the top three are. Cooling, heating and hot water are basically tied.

83
00:06:19,850 --> 00:06:21,695
So what actually moves the needle?

84
00:06:21,995 --> 00:06:31,007
The Department of Energy says setting your thermostat back for at least eight hours a day can save as much as ten percent a year on heating and cooling.

85
00:06:31,307 --> 00:06:37,283
On these average numbers, that's up to about a hundred dollars a year, just for changing a setting.

86
00:06:37,583 --> 00:06:42,479
But there are three things I left off the main list, because most homes don't have them.

87
00:06:42,779 --> 00:06:46,787
A pool pump: about four hundred and thirty-four dollars a year.

88
00:06:47,087 --> 00:06:51,225
Charging an electric car at home: about four hundred and thirty.

89
00:06:51,525 --> 00:06:55,923
And a hot tub, heater plus pump: about three hundred and fifty-seven.

90
00:06:56,223 --> 00:07:00,326
If you have one of these, it belongs right up there with your top three.

91
00:07:00,626 --> 00:07:02,589
Here's the full ranking in one shot.

92
00:07:02,889 --> 00:07:10,803
Fifteen to eleven: washer, microwave, dishwasher, ceiling fans and the stove. All under sixty dollars a year.

93
00:07:11,103 --> 00:07:19,304
Ten to six: the second fridge, the freezer, the main fridge, TVs and lighting. Roughly ninety to a hundred and twenty each.

94
00:07:19,604 --> 00:07:22,794
Five and four: the dryer and the dehumidifier.

95
00:07:23,094 --> 00:07:26,340
And the top three, all around five hundred dollars each.

96
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:28,117
So, here's the whole picture.

97
00:07:28,417 --> 00:07:32,243
Heating, cooling and hot water are where the real money goes.

98
00:07:32,543 --> 00:07:38,268
If your home runs all three on electricity, that's close to fifteen hundred dollars a year.

99
00:07:38,568 --> 00:07:43,752
Lights, TVs and kitchen gadgets are real costs, but small by comparison.

100
00:07:44,052 --> 00:07:49,973
Remember, these are national averages. The only way to know your own numbers is to measure them.

101
00:07:50,273 --> 00:07:57,701
A simple plug-in electricity meter shows exactly what any appliance uses. That's how you find your own surprise.

102
00:07:58,001 --> 00:08:04,379
Next, I'm digging into the top of this list: is a heat pump actually cheaper to run than a gas furnace?

103
00:08:04,679 --> 00:08:06,887
That one is coming up next on this channel.
